-- Passage rg8-comparing-texts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('470e60c2-9a22-544d-bada-45503f550c45', 'rg8-comparing-texts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Three Tries$q$, $q$Text 1: The Three Bridges (a tale)

Long ago, a river divided the village of Marrow from its fields. The village elder asked three brothers to build a bridge before the harvest.

The eldest brother built quickly with reeds. The bridge looked fine, but the first cart of grain pushed it into the water. The middle brother built with heavy stones, but he never asked how the river behaved in spring. The flood carried his stones away.

The youngest brother spent a week sitting on the bank. He watched where the water ran fast and where it ran slow. He asked the old ferryman about floods from years past. Then he built a bridge of timber on tall posts, set where the current was gentle. It stood for a hundred years.

Afterward the villagers liked to say, "The quickest hands are not always the wisest."

Text 2: Prototype Three (a story)

Our science fair team had nine days to build a bridge out of craft sticks that could hold a textbook. Dev glued the first one together in an hour. It snapped under a thin math workbook. Lucia designed the second one with twice as many sticks. It was so heavy that it sagged before we even tested it.

On day four, Amara opened her laptop and found a video about real truss bridges. She sketched triangles in her notebook. She asked her uncle, an engineer, why triangles were so strong. She did not touch the glue for two days.

The third bridge was lighter than Lucia's, and it held three textbooks. Dev wrote the caption for our poster: "Prototype Three: Watch First, Build Second."$q$, 'draft')
