-- Passage rg8-comparing-texts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('59069221-9a6e-51fb-bc52-891a4668a5e0', 'rg8-comparing-texts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Lights for Others$q$, $q$Text 1: The Lantern on the Hill (a tale)

In the mountain town of Selva, the winter roads were dark and full of turns. Every evening an old woman named Ilse climbed the hill above the town and hung a lantern on a tall pole. Travelers far away saw the small light and knew which way to go.

No one paid her. When people asked why she climbed the hill in the cold, she only said, "Someone is always on the road."

One winter, Ilse's knees grew too stiff for the climb. That first evening the hill stayed dark, and the townspeople felt uneasy. Then a boy named Rafi, who had watched her for years, filled the lantern with oil and carried it up the path. He hung it on the pole exactly as she had.

From her window, Ilse saw the light come on. She smiled and went to sleep early.

Text 2: Porch Light (a poem)

My grandmother flips the porch switch
at eight o'clock each night,
though no one we know is coming.

The night nurse from the corner house
walks home at two in the morning.
The bakery driver parks at four.
She says the light is for them,
for anyone between here and there.

This fall her hands shake on the switch.
So I reach past her shoulder
and press it for her, once,
then every night after that.

She does not thank me.
She just turns her chair toward the window
and watches the steps turn gold.$q$, 'draft')
