-- Passage rg8-comparing-texts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5319a908-ba3f-5abb-89c2-79f9f93dd3a1', 'rg8-comparing-texts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keeping the Stories$q$, $q$Text 1: The Story Cloth (a tale)

In a village by the salt marsh lived a weaver named Oona. She could not read or write, and neither could most of her neighbors. Yet she kept the village's history better than anyone.

Each year, Oona wove one new row into a long cloth. A row of blue waves meant the year the great tide reached the doorsteps. A row of small gold squares meant the year the harvest was so large that every family shared grain. When a child asked about the old days, Oona unrolled the cloth and touched each row as she told its story.

When Oona grew old, she taught her grandson to read the rows. "The cloth remembers," she told him, "but only if someone keeps telling it."

Text 2: Episode Twelve (a story)

For her history project, Keiko decided to record her neighbors talking about Pine Avenue. She borrowed a microphone from the school library and knocked on doors all spring.

Mr. Abernathy told her about the summer the creek flooded the basements. Mrs. Nwosu described the block party when the street was first paved. Keiko edited each interview into a short episode and posted it on the class website. She gave each episode a small picture: a wave for the flood, a string of lights for the party.

On the last day of school, Mrs. Nwosu's grandson stopped Keiko in the hall. He had listened to every episode. "Can I record the next one?" he asked. "My grandma has more stories."

Keiko handed him the microphone.$q$, 'draft')
